```cpp
bool JSLexer::error(llvm::SMLoc loc, const llvm::Twine &msg) {
  sm_.error(loc, msg, Subsystem::Lexer);
  if (!sm_.isErrorLimitReached())
    return true;
  forceEOF();
  return false;
}
```

And `isErrorLimitReached()`:
```cpp
bool isErrorLimitReached() const {
    return errorLimitReached_;
}
```

And in SourceErrorManager.cpp:
```cpp
if (LLVM_UNLIKELY(dk == DK_Error && messageCount_[DK_Error] == errorLimit_)) {
    errorLimitReached_ = true;
}
```

So `errorLimitReached_` is set to true when `messageCount_[DK_Error] == errorLimit_`. With error limit = 1, after the first error, `messageCount_[DK_Error]` becomes 1, which equals `errorLimit_` (1), so `errorLimitReached_` is set to true.

But the check in `JSLexer::error()` is:
```cpp
if (!sm_.isErrorLimitReached())
    return true;
```

So on the FIRST error call, `isErrorLimitReached()` returns false (it was just set to true by `sm_.error()`), so `!isErrorLimitReached()` is true, and it returns true WITHOUT calling `forceEOF()`.

On the SECOND error call, `isErrorLimitReached()` returns true, so `forceEOF()` IS called.

So the sequence is:
1. First invalid char in `\u{...}`: `error()` called, error limit reached, but `forceEOF()` NOT called (because `!isErrorLimitReached()` was evaluated before the flag was set? No wait...)

